----------------------------------------------------------------------

   Welcome to the source code of my Neocities site, stored in a
   Git [1] repository!  In case you haven't heard of Git, it is
   essentially a directory content tracker, with fancy metadata
   like the when and why of changes from one state to the next,
   as well as the link between each new state and the old state
   it is based on in said directory.  It's a time machine built
   for files, in other words!  And I love using it for that. =v=

   We are now on GitHub.  Due to help with coding from Claude
   I have exiled myself out of Codeberg.

      https://github.com/rapidcow/neocities        HTTP (Web view!)
      https://github.com/rapidcow/neocities.git    Git clone (HTTP)
      git@github.com:rapidcow/neocities.git        Git clone (SSH)

   You can also get it from me!  though I don't provide a Web view. :(

      https://git.rapidcow.org/pub/www/site/neocities.git
                                                   Git clone only!

   All site content can be re-distributed as long as you credit me
   per CC-BY 4.0! <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>

   All code can be re-distributed or modified under the same terms
   as Perl itself: <https://dev.perl.org/licenses/>

   See LICENSE.Creative and LICENSE.Perl5 for those respectively.

BUILD INSTRUCTIONS
   Building this site depends on siteconf 0.0202, any GenLib, and
   GenGenLib 0.0002.  And of course, a reasonably modern Perl 5.

   You also need Algo-BSearch and IPC-Unix, which you can clone
   with `git submodule init' followed by `git submodule update'.
   You can find more information of my stuff at [2][3][4].

   In many nasty places I took the shortcut of assuming that we
   are on a Unix-like environment (Linux, FreeBSD, OSX/macOS to
   name a few.)  Things may break outside of that assumption...
   (e.g. Windows users may consider WSL.)  To name a few things,
   you need make(1) that runs command in a POSIX shell, with
   shell utilities like cp(1).  Also symlinks should be a thing.

   If you are building the archives you also need GNU tar!
   (But at that point you probably know what you're doing. :)

   [1] https://www.kernel.org/pub/software/scm/git/docs/git.html
   [2] https://foss.rapidcow.org/project/siteconf/
   [3] https://foss.rapidcow.org/lib/perl5/Algo-BSearch/
   [4] https://foss.rapidcow.org/lib/perl5/IPC-Unix/

